# {{.Title}}

This repository is a Tau-ready Python research workspace. It uses `uv` for local Python setup, builds a container image for Tau jobs, and keeps workspace access/quota in `TauWorkspace` instead of committed configs.

Generated from the standalone `tau-gen` workflow, also available as `tau workspace init-repo`.

## Tau authoring strategy

This scaffold intentionally starts with plain Python entrypoints and checked-in
direct Tau configs because repository targets are its initial reusable unit.
Choose the equally supported Python handle path when the project becomes a
code-centric library with local/remote execution, dynamic handle construction,
multi-file staging, or a stable managed one-train-to-one-eval contract. Serving
uses a separately submitted `tau.serve(...)` handle. See the
[Tau config resolution](https://github.com/Azure/taugrid/blob/main/site/content/en/docs/concepts/config-resolution.md).

## Workspace connection

{{if .HasWorkspaceConnection}}The non-secret `tau/workspace.connection.yaml` requires `{{.AuthorizationMode}}` authorization and role `{{.RequiredRole}}`. On first use, Tau obtains AKS cluster-user credentials through your Azure sign-in, verifies that contract, and writes an isolated kubeconfig outside the repository. You do not need a preconfigured context, namespace, queue name, or `--config`.
{{else}}This scaffold is not connected to a Tau workspace yet. Ask the platform owner to add `tau/workspace.connection.yaml`; do not commit kubeconfigs, tokens, namespace names, or queue names.
