## Run on GPUs

`tau run smoke` and `tau run train` are both CPU-only on purpose: they verify
the onboarding path before the platform has committed GPU capacity. Neither one
proves a GPU works.

`tau/train-gpu.yaml` is the GPU counterpart:

```bash
tau run --config tau/train-gpu.yaml --dry-run=client   # inspect the rendered pod
tau run train-gpu                                       # submit
```

`policy.shape` is the field that requests GPUs — `1x` renders
`nvidia.com/gpu: 1`, `8x` requests a full node. Everything else about the config
matches `tau/train.yaml`.

Three things are platform-owned, so ask before assuming them:

| Need | Confirm with |
|---|---|
| GPU capacity and quota in your queue | `tau workspace status {{.Workspace}}` |
| Which GPU classes exist (to set `policy.gpu_class`) | ask the platform owner |
| The `/data` PVC, if you uncomment `storage.data_pvc` | `tau workspace check {{.Workspace}}` |

If a GPU pod stays `Pending`, it is almost always queue quota, a `gpu_class`
that matches no node, or a missing PVC — not the config itself.

`tau run smoke` is the reserved platform onboarding smoke. To run this
repository's project-specific smoke config, use
`tau run --config tau/smoke.yaml`.

## What each step actually proves

These are different checks, and they fail for different reasons. Run them in
order; each one adds exactly one new thing that can break.

| Command | Proves | Does not prove |
|---|---|---|
| `tau run smoke` | Workspace, queue admission, and pod execution work. Runs a public base image and a trivial command. | Nothing about your image, GPUs, or storage. |
| `tau run --config tau/smoke.yaml` | Your container image pulls and runs. This is where private-registry (ACR) pull problems surface. | That your training code works. |
| `tau run train` | Your training entrypoint runs on CPU. | Anything GPU-specific. |
| `tau run train-gpu` | Your code runs on a real GPU. | Durable `/data` storage, unless you enable `storage.data_pvc`. |

A green `tau run smoke` therefore does not mean "my code works" — it means the
platform is reachable. If `tau run smoke` passes but
`tau run --config tau/smoke.yaml` fails, suspect the image or registry
credentials, not the cluster.

